\begin_layout Title
Converting Hospitals From Not-For-Profit to For-Profit Status
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
February 18, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A majority of American hospitals are organized as charitable, not-for-profit
 entities.
 Their main objective is not to maximize profit, but instead, increase community
 health care and charitable goals.
 Since these hospitals are non-profit organizations, many laws and provisions
 make them tax exempt.
 However, there are now many hospitals that are now converting to for-profit
 corporations.
 Obviously, the conversion of hospitals from non-profit to for-profit organizati
ons, can have major policy implications, especially in regards to health
 outcomes and expenditures.
 There is also the possibility of conducting pertinent IO research in regards
 to the changes in pricing, expenditures of hospitals, and overall provision
 of services.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there only a few papers offering some details concerning hospital
 conversion.
 Gray (1997) provides a healthy policy framework for assessing conversions
 of hospitals and HMOs.
 The paper discusses the policy implications of the process, and it addresses
 the differences in structure, organization, and the social benefits of
 non-profit versus for-profit hospitals.
 Cutler and Horwitz (2000) provide a preliminary discussion of the cause
 and effects of conversion by examining two specific case studies.
 After searching online, I did not find any extensive empirical research
 on the reasons for and the effects of hospital conversions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

My paper will primarily focus on the effect conversion has on the expenditures
 of hospitals and the prices they charge for high demand medical procedures.
 So, I will need to collect data on the pricing and expenditures of individual
 hospitals.
 In order to obtain some of this pertinent data, I propose accessing the
 
\bar under
Texas Census Research Data Center (TCRDC)
\bar default
.
 The data center contains major health datasets, including the Agency for
 Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ), the Medical Expenditure Panel Survey
 (MEPS), and more.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The empirical strategy I propose is a differences-in-differences design.
 In order for this design to succeed, I propose finding hospitals that are
 fairly close together: either they are located in the same general region
 or they can serve a majority of the same population.
 Thus, the treatment group consists of hospitals that have converted from
 non-profit to for-profit corporations, while the control group are hospitals
 in the same area that remain non-profit organizations.
 The key identifying assumption is that absent the treatment, the changes
 in hospital pricing and expenditures would have been the same over time
 for the treatment and control groups.
 In the estimating equation, I will control for population demographics,
 time fixed effects, region fixed effects, and variables associated with
 hospital characteristics.
\end_layout

